        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Heart Rate Zones", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I calculate my maximum heart rate?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The common formula is 220 minus your age, though 208 minus 0.7 times age fits better across ages. Both are population averages: individual maximums routinely sit ten to twelve beats either side, and twenty-beat differences are unremarkable. Treat any figure as a starting hypothesis and adjust it against how sessions actually feel.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What heart rate zone should I train in on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Roughly four-fifths of your time in zones 1 to 2 — easy enough for full sentences — with the remainder in zones 4 to 5, and comparatively little in zone 3. Most people invert this, spending nearly everything at moderate intensity, which accumulates fatigue without a proportionate training effect.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are treadmill heart rate grip sensors accurate?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not very, and least so when it matters. They need still, firm contact that running does not allow, they degrade as hands get damp, and they are worst at high intensity. They also require holding the rails, which transfers weight to your arms and changes the workout you are measuring. Use a chest strap instead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is zone 2 training and why does everyone talk about it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Zone 2 is easy aerobic work at roughly 60 to 70% of maximum heart rate, where you can hold full sentences. It builds the aerobic base, generates little fatigue so it can be done often, and is the intensity most amateurs neglect. The overreach is treating the boundary as precise when the underlying maximum is an estimate.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my heart rate rise during a steady treadmill run?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Cardiac drift — heart rate climbs over a long session at unchanged pace, largely from rising core temperature and falling blood volume. A five-to-ten-beat rise over an hour is normal. Indoors it is more pronounced because heat dissipates poorly, so a fan makes a measurable difference.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do heart rate zones work if I take beta blockers?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Beta blockers substantially blunt heart rate response, so percentage-of-maximum zones do not represent your effort and the calculations on this page do not apply. Your target ranges need to come from your doctor. Perceived effort and the talk test remain usable in the meantime.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
