          <a href="#confounders" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Things That Move Your Heart Rate That Are Not Effort</a>
          <a href="#talk-test" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. The Talk Test, Which Is Free and Often Better</a>
          <a href="#using-zones" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Using Zones on a Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#building-a-week" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. A Week Built on Zones</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. The Short Version</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Heart rate zones are a genuinely useful way to structure training, built on a foundation
          shakier than most people realise. The framework is sound; the number it is anchored to
          is a population average that fits a large minority of individuals badly.
        </p>

        <div id="what-zones-are" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Zones Are For</h2>
<p>
            Heart rate is a proxy for how hard your cardiovascular system is working. Zones divide
            that range into bands, each associated with a different training effect, so you can
            aim for a specific adaptation rather than simply working "quite hard".
          </p>
          <p>
            The genuine value is not precision. It is that zones make you go easy on easy days.
            Left to instinct, most people train everything at a moderate intensity — hard enough
            to accumulate fatigue, not hard enough to drive adaptation. A number on a screen
            telling you that you are above your easy ceiling is an effective corrective, and that
            is most of what zone training actually delivers.
          </p>
          <p>
            The framework's weakness is that every zone is calculated from your maximum heart
            rate, and almost nobody knows theirs. What most people use is an estimate, and the
            estimate is poor for individuals — which is the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="max-heart-rate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Problem With 220 Minus Age</h2>
<p>
            The familiar formula — maximum heart rate equals 220 minus your age — is a population
            average derived from fitting a line through a wide scatter of individuals. It was never
            intended as an individual prescription.
          </p>
          <p>
            The scatter is substantial. Individual maximum heart rates commonly sit ten to twelve
            beats either side of the formula's prediction, and outliers of twenty beats or more
            are unremarkable. For a 40-year-old the formula says 180; the real figure might be
            165 or 195, and both are entirely normal.
          </p>
          <p>
            That matters because every zone boundary is a percentage of that number. If your true
            maximum is 195 and you are training to a predicted 180, your "easy" zone is genuinely
            easy and you are probably under-training. If your true maximum is 165, your "easy"
            zone is moderate work and you are quietly over-reaching every session.
          </p>
          <p>
            Alternative formulas exist — 208 minus 0.7 times age is generally considered a better
            fit across ages — and they are better averages with the same individual problem. There
            is no formula that knows you.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical answer: treat any calculated zone as a starting hypothesis, then adjust
            it against how you actually feel. If your "easy" zone leaves you breathless, it is
            wrong for you regardless of the arithmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="five-zones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Five Zones</h2>
<p>
            Percentages are of maximum heart rate. Treat the boundaries as blurred rather than
            precise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Zone 1 — 50-60%. Very easy.</strong> Warm-up, cool-down and recovery walking.
            Conversation is completely unaffected. Useful and often skipped.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Zone 2 — 60-70%. Easy aerobic.</strong> Full sentences without effort. This is
            where the majority of training should happen and where most people spend too little
            time, because it feels insufficiently productive.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Zone 3 — 70-80%. Moderate.</strong> Short sentences. Comfortable but clearly
            working. The zone people default to for everything, which is the core problem in
            amateur training.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Zone 4 — 80-90%. Hard.</strong> A few words at a time. Threshold and interval
            territory, sustainable for perhaps twenty to forty minutes at the lower end and far
            less at the top.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Zone 5 — 90-100%. Maximal.</strong> Single words or none. Short intervals only,
            and a small fraction of any week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="zone-two" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Why Zone 2 Gets All the Attention</h2>
<p>
            Zone 2 has become the most discussed band in amateur endurance training, and the
            enthusiasm is broadly justified even if the presentation is often overheated.
          </p>
          <p>
            The case for it is straightforward. Easy aerobic work builds the cardiovascular base
            that everything else depends on, it can be done frequently because it generates little
            fatigue, and it is the intensity most amateur athletes systematically neglect in
            favour of moderate work that is harder without being productive.
          </p>
          <p>
            The overreach is treating the boundary as a precise physiological threshold that must
            not be crossed. Given that the underlying maximum heart rate is an estimate with a
            ten-beat error bar, agonising over three beats is misplaced precision. Anxiously
            watching a monitor to stay under a number derived from a population average is not
            better training.
          </p>
          <p>
            The useful version: <strong>most of your week should be genuinely easy.</strong> On a
            treadmill that usually means walking with some incline, or running slower than feels
            satisfying. Whether the monitor says 68% or 72% matters far less than whether you
            could hold a conversation, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">training guide</a>
            builds a week on that principle without needing a monitor at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grip-sensors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Why Treadmill Grip Sensors Mislead</h2>
<p>
            The metal handles on a treadmill's handrails read heart rate by detecting the
            electrical signal across your palms. It works in principle and poorly in practice, in
            ways that matter most exactly when you want the number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>They need still, firm, consistent contact</strong>, which running does not
            provide. Readings drift and jump as grip pressure changes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Damp hands change conductivity</strong>, so accuracy degrades as a session
            progresses — precisely when you are most interested.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>They are worst at high intensity</strong>, where movement and sweat are
            greatest, which is when zone boundaries matter most.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Holding the rails changes the workout.</strong> This is the serious one. To
            use grip sensors you must hold on, and holding on transfers weight to your arms and the
            frame — particularly on incline. So the act of measuring alters what you are
            measuring, and makes the session easier than the console reports.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are training by heart rate, a chest strap is a genuinely different class of
            measurement and an optical wrist monitor is a reasonable middle ground. Grip sensors
            are best treated as a rough spot check while walking, not as a training instrument.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="confounders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Things That Move Your Heart Rate That Are Not Effort</h2>
<p>
            A given pace does not produce a given heart rate. Several factors shift it by ten
            beats or more without any change in how hard you are working.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cardiac drift.</strong> During a long steady session heart rate rises over time
            at unchanged pace, largely from rising core temperature and falling blood volume. Over
            an hour a five-to-ten-beat rise is normal, so chasing a fixed number means slowing down
            for no good reason.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heat and airflow.</strong> Indoors without moving air, body heat accumulates
            faster than outdoors, so treadmill heart rate at a given pace often runs higher. A fan
            makes a measurable difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Caffeine, dehydration, illness and poor sleep</strong> all raise it. So does
            stress. An elevated heart rate at your normal easy pace is often information about your
            recovery rather than your fitness.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Medication.</strong> Beta blockers in particular blunt heart rate response
            substantially, which makes percentage-of-maximum zones meaningless. If you take
            anything affecting heart rate, your targets need to come from your doctor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="talk-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Talk Test, Which Is Free and Often Better</h2>
<p>
            Breathing is a self-calibrating intensity gauge. It costs nothing, needs no battery,
            and automatically accounts for heat, fatigue, illness and everything else in the
            previous section.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Full sentences comfortably</strong> — zones 1 to 2. Easy. Most of your week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Short sentences</strong> — zone 3. Moderate. Useful in small amounts and
            over-used by almost everyone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A few words at a time</strong> — zone 4. Hard. Once or twice a week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Single words or none</strong> — zone 5. Maximal. Short intervals only.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most people on a treadmill, this is sufficient and arguably superior. It is
            immune to a mis-estimated maximum heart rate, unaffected by damp hands, and it does not
            require holding the rails. If you are not training for a specific event, you can run a
            well-structured week on breathing alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="using-zones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Using Zones on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            If you do want to train by heart rate, a few treadmill-specific points make it work
            better.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use incline rather than speed to raise heart rate.</strong> You can reach the
            same zone at a walking pace on a grade as at a running pace on the flat, with far less
            impact — the most useful thing a treadmill offers a heart-rate-guided trainer. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and incline chart</a>
            covers the equivalences.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Allow a lag.</strong> Heart rate takes one to three minutes to stabilise after
            a change in effort. Adjust the machine, then wait before judging whether the setting
            was right — chasing the number produces a sawtooth of over-corrections.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Set a ceiling rather than a target for easy sessions.</strong> "Stay under 140"
            works better than "hold 135", because it removes the temptation to speed up on a day
            when your heart rate is naturally low.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use a fan.</strong> It reduces heat-driven drift meaningfully and makes zone
            work far more consistent indoors.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not use grip sensors for interval sessions.</strong> They are least accurate
            exactly there, and holding the rails during intervals is unsafe as well as
            counterproductive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="building-a-week" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. A Week Built on Zones</h2>
<p>
            The principle is the same one that underpins all endurance training, expressed in
            heart rate terms: <strong>roughly four-fifths of your time in zones 1 to 2, and the
            remainder in zones 4 to 5, with relatively little in zone 3.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            That distribution feels wrong to most people, because zone 3 is the intensity that
            feels productive. It is also the intensity that generates meaningful fatigue without a
            proportionate adaptation, which is why "moderate everything" is the most common
            plateau in amateur training.
          </p>
          <p>
            A workable four-session week: two easy zone 2 sessions of 30 to 45 minutes, one incline
            session held in zone 2 to low zone 3, and one interval session with the efforts in zone
            4. That is one genuinely hard session out of four.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you only take one thing from this page: <strong>make the easy sessions
            easier.</strong> Almost everyone's zone 2 is actually zone 3, and correcting that
            single error does more than any refinement of interval structure. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a>
            covers the hard end once the easy end is right.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. The Short Version</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Zones are a useful framework on a shaky foundation.</strong> The 220-minus-age
            estimate is a population average that misses individuals by ten to twelve beats
            routinely, so treat any calculated zone as a hypothesis and adjust it by feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not rely on treadmill grip sensors.</strong> They are least accurate at high
            intensity, degrade as your hands get damp, and require holding the rails — which
            changes the workout you are trying to measure. Use a chest strap if the number matters.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The talk test is free and often better.</strong> Full sentences means easy;
            single words means hard. It self-calibrates for heat, fatigue and illness in a way no
            formula does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And if you take medication affecting heart rate or have a cardiac condition,
            none of the arithmetic here applies to you.</strong> Ask your doctor for target ranges.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate my maximum heart rate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The common formula is 220 minus your age, though 208 minus 0.7 times age fits better across ages. Both are population averages: individual maximums routinely sit ten to twelve beats either side, and twenty-beat differences are unremarkable. Treat any figure as a starting hypothesis and adjust it against how sessions actually feel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What heart rate zone should I train in on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly four-fifths of your time in zones 1 to 2 — easy enough for full sentences — with the remainder in zones 4 to 5, and comparatively little in zone 3. Most people invert this, spending nearly everything at moderate intensity, which accumulates fatigue without a proportionate training effect.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are treadmill heart rate grip sensors accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not very, and least so when it matters. They need still, firm contact that running does not allow, they degrade as hands get damp, and they are worst at high intensity. They also require holding the rails, which transfers weight to your arms and changes the workout you are measuring. Use a chest strap instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is zone 2 training and why does everyone talk about it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Zone 2 is easy aerobic work at roughly 60 to 70% of maximum heart rate, where you can hold full sentences. It builds the aerobic base, generates little fatigue so it can be done often, and is the intensity most amateurs neglect. The overreach is treating the boundary as precise when the underlying maximum is an estimate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my heart rate rise during a steady treadmill run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cardiac drift — heart rate climbs over a long session at unchanged pace, largely from rising core temperature and falling blood volume. A five-to-ten-beat rise over an hour is normal. Indoors it is more pronounced because heat dissipates poorly, so a fan makes a measurable difference.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do heart rate zones work if I take beta blockers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Beta blockers substantially blunt heart rate response, so percentage-of-maximum zones do not represent your effort and the calculations on this page do not apply. Your target ranges need to come from your doctor. Perceived effort and the talk test remain usable in the meantime.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Heart Rate Training</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first is the reason most heart-rate-guided training fails to deliver, and it is the easiest to fix.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Making easy sessions moderate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Almost everyone's zone 2 is really zone 3 — hard enough to accumulate fatigue, not hard enough to drive adaptation. Correcting this single error does more than any refinement of interval structure. On a treadmill that usually means walking with incline, or running slower than feels satisfying.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a formula-derived maximum as fact</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Individual maximum heart rates sit ten to twelve beats either side of 220-minus-age routinely. If your calculated easy zone leaves you breathless, the calculation is wrong for you, not your body. Adjust the zones against perceived effort rather than trusting the arithmetic over your own breathing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Training by grip sensor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">They are least accurate at high intensity, drift as hands get damp, and require holding the handrails — which transfers load to your arms and makes the session easier than the console reports. That means the act of measuring changes what is measured. Use a chest strap, or use the talk test.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chasing the number in real time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Heart rate takes one to three minutes to stabilise after an effort change, so adjusting the machine every time the display moves produces a sawtooth of over-corrections. Change the setting, wait a couple of minutes, then judge. For easy sessions, set a ceiling rather than a target.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

